//? Title: Convolution Layers as Matrices: Shapes, Transposes, Receptive Fields and Spectra
//? Subtitle: Output-size rules, every convolution layer checked against its explicit matrix, the transposed convolution and its checkerboard, receptive and effective receptive fields, the singular values of a convolution from the Fourier transform of its kernel, and convolutional networks against a dense network on digits, using datamunge's nn::conv2d family, nn::conv2d_matrix, nn::receptive_fields and nn::conv2d_circular_singular_values
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A convolution layer is a linear map with a sparse matrix of repeated entries. This report builds that matrix with a new function, `nn::conv2d_matrix`, and uses it as an independent reference for every
convolution layer in datamunge: 1-D, 2-D, depthwise, separable, dilated and transposed. All agree with their matrices to \(4\times10^{-16}\) or better, and a 3-D convolution agrees with plain loops. The
transposed convolution is the matrix's transpose, and its checkerboard pattern is the column sums of the matrix's sparsity pattern, uniform exactly when the stride divides the kernel size. Receptive
fields from the size-and-jump recursion match the support of one unit's input gradient for five stacks. The effective receptive field of \(n\) random \(3\times3\) layers has standard deviation
\(\sqrt{2n/3}\), as a path-counting argument predicts, against a receptive-field half-width of \(n\). The singular values of a circular convolution, computed from \(64\) small Fourier-domain matrices, match a dense SVD to \(10^{-14}\).
On digits, after a short run of 500 Adam steps, a 5130-parameter convolutional network reaches 89.1% against 90.6% for a dense network of similar size. When the test images move two pixels, the convolutional networks keep 32–57% where the dense network falls to 13–17%.
)

\toc

> The problem

Convolution layers apply the same small filter at every position of an image or sequence. They are usually described by their shapes and their output-size rules. Behind those rules is
plain linear algebra: for fixed weights, a convolution is multiplication by a matrix, and nearly everything about it can be read from that matrix. The transposed convolution is its
transpose, the receptive field is the set of nonzero columns in a row, and how much the layer can amplify a signal is its largest singular value. The matrix is large and sparse, so
libraries never form it, and that makes it a good independent check.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.2_reusable_layer_library/conv_layers.cpp`. That example checked output sizes, compared the 1-D, 2-D, 3-D, depthwise and separable
layers with hand-written loops, counted parameters, showed that the transposed convolution is the input gradient, printed the checkerboard of overlaps and showed that a dilated kernel is a larger kernel with
holes. All of these are kept here, most of them checked against the explicit matrix, with the theory of receptive fields and singular values added.

> The method

>> Output sizes

A 2-D convolution in datamunge takes an input \(x\) of shape \([N,H,W,C_{\text{in}}]\) and a kernel \(K\) of shape \([k_h,k_w,C_{\text{in}},C_{\text{out}}]\), and computes the cross-correlation

$$
y_{n,i,j,o}=\sum_{a,b,q}K_{a,b,q,o}\,x_{n,\;is+ad-p_h,\;js+bd-p_w,\;q},
$$
\alttext(y at example n, row i, column j and output channel o equals the sum over a, b and q of K a b q o times x at example n, row i s plus a d minus p h, column j s plus b d minus p w, channel q.)

with stride \(s\), dilation \(d\), padding before the data \(p\), and \(x=0\) outside the image. A dilated kernel covers \(k_{\text{eff}}=d(k-1)+1\) positions. Along an axis of length \(L\), VALID padding (none) gives
\(\lfloor(L-k_{\text{eff}})/s\rfloor+1\) outputs and SAME padding gives \(\lceil L/s\rceil\), with \(\max(0,(\lceil L/s\rceil-1)s+k_{\text{eff}}-L)\) zeros added and the odd one placed after the data, as in TensorFlow. A depthwise
convolution filters each input channel separately with \(M\) filters, and a separable convolution is a depthwise one followed by a \(1\times1\) convolution. A \(k\times k\) separable layer from \(C_{\text{in}}\) to
\(C_{\text{out}}\) channels has \(k^2C_{\text{in}}+C_{\text{in}}C_{\text{out}}\) weights instead of \(k^2C_{\text{in}}C_{\text{out}}\), a ratio of \(1/C_{\text{out}}+1/k^2\) (Chollet, 2017; Howard et al., 2017).

>> The convolution matrix

Flatten one example's input and output in row-major order. The equation above is then \(\operatorname{vec}(y)=M\operatorname{vec}(x)\), where the row of output \((i,j,o)\) holds \(K_{a,b,q,o}\) in the column
of input \((is+ad-p_h,\,js+bd-p_w,\,q)\) when that position is inside the image, and zeros elsewhere. For stride 1 the entry depends only on the offset between the input and output positions, so
\(M\) is doubly block Toeplitz: block Toeplitz across rows of the image, each block Toeplitz across columns. `nn::conv2d_matrix` builds \(M\) for any strides, dilations, groups and padding. A depthwise
convolution is a grouped one with one group per input channel, and a separable convolution's matrix is the product of its pointwise and depthwise matrices. With the option `circular`, positions
outside the image wrap around instead of reading zeros.

The gradient of a loss \(L\) with respect to the input is \(\operatorname{vec}(\partial L/\partial x)=M^\top\operatorname{vec}(\partial L/\partial y)\). This map is the transposed convolution, used as a learned upsampler.
Each input pixel of a transposed convolution spreads a copy of the kernel over the output, and adjacent copies are \(s\) apart. With a ones input and a ones kernel, output position \(t\) along one axis
receives either \(\lfloor k/s\rfloor\) or \(\lceil k/s\rceil\) copies, so the counts are uniform only when \(s\) divides \(k\). In 2-D the count is a product of two such numbers. Uneven counts give the checkerboard
artefacts of generated images (Odena, Dumoulin and Olah, 2016). These counts are the column sums of the 0/1 pattern of \(M\).

>> Receptive fields

The receptive field of a unit is the set of input positions that can affect it, which is the set of nonzero columns in its row of the product of the layer matrices. Along one axis, a stack of layers
with kernels \(k_l\), strides \(s_l\) and dilations \(d_l\) gives

$$
r_l=r_{l-1}+(k_{\text{eff},l}-1)\,j_{l-1},\qquad j_l=j_{l-1}s_l,\qquad r_0=j_0=1,
$$
\alttext(r l equals r l minus 1 plus k effective l minus 1, times j l minus 1. j l equals j l minus 1 times s l. r 0 and j 0 are both 1.)

where \(j_l\) is the distance in the input between neighbouring units of layer \(l\) (Dumoulin and Visin, 2016). Pooling layers count as layers with their window and stride. `nn::receptive_fields` returns
\((r_l,j_l)\) for every layer. Dilations \(1,2,4,\dots,2^{m-1}\) with \(3\times3\) kernels give \(r=2^{m+1}-1\), exponential growth at no extra cost in parameters (Yu and Koltun, 2016).

Not every position in the receptive field matters equally. Take \(n\) stride-1 \(3\times3\) layers, without nonlinearities, with independent zero-mean weights of variance \(\sigma^2\). The gradient of one output with respect to an
input pixel is a sum over paths through the layers of products of weights. Two different paths differ in at least one weight, which appears once in their product, so the cross terms vanish in
expectation and

$$
\mathbb E\Big[\Big(\frac{\partial y}{\partial x_{u}}\Big)^2\Big]\propto\#\{\text{paths from }u\text{ to the output}\}.
$$
\alttext(The expected square of the derivative of y with respect to input pixel u is proportional to the number of paths from u to the output.)

The number of paths is the convolution of \(n\) copies of the \(3\times3\) box. Along each axis it is the distribution of a sum of \(n\) independent steps uniform on \(\{-1,0,1\}\), each with variance \(2/3\). The
squared gradient therefore has standard deviation \(\sqrt{2n/3}\) per axis and tends to a Gaussian, while the receptive field has half-width \(n\). The share of the receptive field that matters shrinks like
\(1/\sqrt n\): this is the effective receptive field of Luo et al. (2016). With all weights equal to 1 the gradient is the path count itself, and its square, close to a squared Gaussian, has standard deviation
about \(\sqrt{n/3}\).

>> Singular values from the Fourier transform

With circular padding and stride 1, the matrix commutes with every circular shift of the image. The 2-D discrete Fourier transform therefore block-diagonalises it, and

$$
M=(F^*\otimes I)\,\operatorname{diag}_{u,v}\big(\hat K(u,v)^\top\big)\,(F\otimes I),\qquad
\hat K(u,v)=\sum_{a,b}K_{a,b}\,e^{-2\pi i(au/H+bv/W)},
$$
\alttext(M equals F star Kronecker I, times the block diagonal matrix of K hat of u v transposed, times F Kronecker I, where K hat of u v is the sum over a and b of K a b times e to the minus 2 pi i times a u over H plus b v over W.)

where \(F\) is the unitary 2-D DFT, \(K_{a,b}\) is the \(C_{\text{in}}\times C_{\text{out}}\) matrix of tap \((a,b)\), and the sign and conjugation conventions do not change singular values. The singular values of
\(M\) are thus the union, over the \(HW\) frequencies, of the singular values of the small complex matrices \(\hat K(u,v)\) (Sedghi, Gupta and Long, 2019). `nn::conv2d_circular_singular_values` computes them
through the real form \(\begin{pmatrix}\operatorname{Re}\hat K&-\operatorname{Im}\hat K\\\operatorname{Im}\hat K&\operatorname{Re}\hat K\end{pmatrix}\), which has each singular value of \(\hat K\) twice. That costs \(HW\) decompositions of size \(2C\) instead of one of size \(HWC\).

Two identities check the result. Each weight appears \(HW\) times in the circular matrix, so \(\sum\sigma_i^2=\|M\|_F^2=HW\|K\|_F^2\). Each tap contributes a shift matrix, which is a permutation for circular padding and a partial permutation for
zero padding, times \(K_{a,b}\). The triangle inequality then bounds the spectral norm of either matrix by \(\sum_{a,b}\|K_{a,b}\|_2\). This is a cheap bound used in Lipschitz estimates.

> Simple examples

>> Shapes, every layer against its matrix, and parameter counts

The program prints the output lengths for an input of length 7 and kernel 3 from the closed forms, from `nn::conv_output_size` and from a `conv1d` layer. It then compares each layer with an independent
reference. The 2-D, 1-D, depthwise, separable and dilated layers are compared with `nn::conv2d_matrix`, applied by a matrix product. The 3-D layer is compared with loops, and the transposed convolution with the
autograd input gradient and with \(M^\top\). Last come the parameter counts from the layers' stores. Everything runs in float64.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Padding;
using nn::Tensor;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

// Applies a convolution matrix M [rows, H W Cin] to every example of x [N, H, W, Cin].
Tensor apply(const Tensor& M, const Tensor& x) {
  const std::int64_t n = x.size(0);
  return nn::matmul(nn::reshape(x, {n, x.numel() / n}), nn::transpose(M, {1, 0}));
}
double max_diff(const Tensor& a, const Tensor& b) {
  return nn::max(nn::abs(nn::reshape(a, {a.numel()}) - nn::reshape(b, {b.numel()}))).item();
}
const char* pad_name(Padding p) { return p == Padding::Valid ? "VALID" : "SAME"; }

int main() {
  nn::Generator gen(1);
  // 1. Output length for input length 7 and kernel 3: closed forms, conv_output_size and a conv1d layer.
  std::print("| padding | stride | dilation | closed form | conv_output_size | conv1d output |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (Padding pad : {Padding::Valid, Padding::Same})
    for (int s : {1, 2, 3})
      for (int d : {1, 2}) {
        if (s > 1 && d > 1) continue;
        const int keff = d * 2 + 1;
        const int closed = pad == Padding::Valid ? (7 - keff) / s + 1 : (7 + s - 1) / s;
        nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
        const Tensor y = nn::conv1d(p, "c", nn::zeros({1, 7, 1}, F64), 1, 3, {.stride = s, .dilation = d, .padding = pad, .bias = false});
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", pad_name(pad), s, d, closed, nn::conv_output_size(7, 3, s, d, pad), y.size(1));
      }

  // 2. Every layer against an independent reference: the convolution matrix from nn::conv2d_matrix, or loops for 3-D.
  std::print("\n| layer | input | output | reference | max abs. difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  auto shape_str = [](const Tensor& t) {
    std::string s = "[";
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.shape().size(); ++i) s += (i ? ", " : "") + std::to_string(t.shape()[i]);
    return s + "]";
  };
  const Tensor x = nn::normal({2, 6, 5, 3}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
  for (Padding pad : {Padding::Valid, Padding::Same})
    for (int s : {1, 2})
      for (int d : {1, 2}) {
        if (s > 1 && d > 1) continue;
        nn::ParameterStore p(2, F64);
        const Tensor y = nn::conv2d(p, "c", x, 4, 3, {.stride = s, .dilation = d, .padding = pad, .bias = false});
        const Tensor M = nn::conv2d_matrix(6, 5, p.at("c/W"), nn::conv_options(s, pad, d));
        std::print("| conv2d 3×3, {}, stride {}, dilation {} | {} | {} | matrix | {:.1e} |\n", pad_name(pad), s, d, shape_str(x), shape_str(y), max_diff(y, apply(M, x)));
      }
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(3, F64);
    const Tensor x1 = nn::normal({2, 9, 2}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    const Tensor y1 = nn::conv1d(p, "c", x1, 3, 3, {.padding = Padding::Valid, .bias = false});
    const Tensor M = nn::conv2d_matrix(1, 9, p.at("c/W"), nn::conv_options(1, Padding::Valid));
    std::print("| conv1d, kernel 3, VALID | {} | {} | matrix (height 1) | {:.1e} |\n", shape_str(x1), shape_str(y1), max_diff(y1, apply(M, nn::reshape(x1, {2, 1, 9, 2}))));
    const Tensor x3 = nn::normal({1, 4, 4, 4, 2}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    const Tensor y3 = nn::conv3d(p, "c3", x3, 3, 2, {.padding = Padding::Valid, .bias = false});
    const Tensor W3 = p.at("c3/W");
    double err = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
      for (int j = 0; j < 3; ++j)
        for (int l = 0; l < 3; ++l)
          for (int o = 0; o < 3; ++o) {
            double acc = 0;
            for (int a = 0; a < 2; ++a)
              for (int b = 0; b < 2; ++b)
                for (int c = 0; c < 2; ++c)
                  for (int ci = 0; ci < 2; ++ci) acc += x3.at({0, i + a, j + b, l + c, ci}) * W3.at({a, b, c, ci, o});
            err = std::max(err, std::abs(acc - y3.at({0, i, j, l, o})));
          }
    std::print("| conv3d, kernel 2, VALID | {} | {} | loops | {:.1e} |\n", shape_str(x3), shape_str(y3), err);
  }
  const Tensor x6 = nn::normal({1, 6, 6, 3}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(4, F64);
    const Tensor dw = nn::depthwise_conv2d(p, "dw", x6, 2, 3, {.bias = false});
    // Depthwise = grouped convolution with one group per input channel: [3, 3, C, M] -> [3, 3, 1, C M].
    const Tensor Mdw = nn::conv2d_matrix(6, 6, nn::reshape(p.at("dw/W"), {3, 3, 1, 6}), {.groups = 3});
    std::print("| depthwise 3×3, multiplier 2 | {} | {} | grouped matrix | {:.1e} |\n", shape_str(x6), shape_str(dw), max_diff(dw, apply(Mdw, x6)));
    const Tensor sep = nn::separable_conv2d(p, "sep", x6, 8, 3, {.bias = false});
    const Tensor Md = nn::conv2d_matrix(6, 6, nn::reshape(p.at("sep/depthwise/W"), {3, 3, 1, 3}), {.groups = 3});
    const Tensor Mp = nn::conv2d_matrix(6, 6, p.at("sep/pointwise/W"));
    std::print("| separable 3×3 to 8 channels | {} | {} | pointwise × depthwise matrices | {:.1e} |\n", shape_str(x6), shape_str(sep), max_diff(sep, apply(nn::matmul(Mp, Md), x6)));
  }
  {
    // A dilated 3x3 kernel against an ordinary 5x5 kernel with holes: compare the layers and the matrices.
    nn::ParameterStore p(7, F64);
    const Tensor x8 = nn::normal({1, 8, 8, 1}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    const Tensor y = nn::conv2d(p, "d", x8, 1, 3, {.dilation = 2, .bias = false});
    std::vector<double> w5(25, 0.0);
    for (int a = 0; a < 3; ++a)
      for (int b = 0; b < 3; ++b) w5[2 * a * 5 + 2 * b] = p.at("d/W").at({a, b, 0, 0});
    const Tensor W5 = nn::from_vector(w5, {5, 5, 1, 1});
    const double dm_ = max_diff(nn::conv2d_matrix(8, 8, p.at("d/W"), nn::conv_options(1, Padding::Same, 2)), nn::conv2d_matrix(8, 8, W5));
    std::print("| conv2d 3×3, dilation 2, SAME | {} | {} | 5×5 kernel with holes (layer; matrix) | {:.1e}; {:.1e} |\n", shape_str(x8), shape_str(y), max_diff(y, nn::conv2d(x8, W5)), dm_);
  }
  {
    // The transposed convolution is the gradient of the convolution with respect to its input, i.e. M^T applied to dL/dy.
    nn::ParameterStore p(5, F64);
    Tensor xg = nn::normal({1, 6, 6, 2}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    const Tensor r = nn::normal({1, 3, 3, 3}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    xg.requires_grad_();
    const Tensor y = nn::conv2d(p, "c", xg, 3, 3, {.stride = 2, .bias = false});
    const Tensor gx = nn::grad(nn::sum(y * r), {xg})[0];
    const Tensor W = p.at("c/W").detach();
    const Tensor t = nn::conv2d_transpose(r, W, {1, 6, 6, 2}, nn::conv_options(2));
    const Tensor MT = nn::reshape(nn::matmul(nn::reshape(r, {1, 27}), nn::conv2d_matrix(6, 6, W, nn::conv_options(2))), {1, 6, 6, 2});
    std::print("| conv2d_transpose, stride 2 | {} | {} | autograd input gradient; Mᵀ r | {:.1e}; {:.1e} |\n", shape_str(r), shape_str(t), max_diff(t, gx), max_diff(t, MT));
  }

  // 3. Parameter counts from the layers' stores.
  std::print("\n| layer | parameters | formula |\n| :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(0, F64);
    (void)nn::conv2d(p, "c", x, 4, 3);
    std::print("| conv2d 3×3, 3 → 4 channels, with bias | {} | 3·3·3·4 + 4 |\n", p.count());
    const Tensor z = nn::zeros({1, 4, 4, 64}, F64);
    nn::ParameterStore full(0, F64), sep(0, F64);
    (void)nn::conv2d(full, "f", z, 128, 3, {.bias = false});
    (void)nn::separable_conv2d(sep, "s", z, 128, 3, {.bias = false});
    std::print("| conv2d 3×3, 64 → 128, no bias | {} | 3·3·64·128 |\n", full.count());
    std::print("| separable 3×3, 64 → 128, no bias | {} | 3·3·64 + 64·128 |\n", sep.count());
    std::print("\nRatio separable / full: {:.4f}; 1/C_out + 1/k² = {:.4f}.\n", static_cast<double>(sep.count()) / full.count(), 1.0 / 128 + 1.0 / 9);
  }
}
```
