Ratio separable / full: 0.1189; 1/C_out + 1/k² = 0.1189.
// result_end

The closed forms, `nn::conv_output_size` and the layer agree on all eight output lengths, which are the original example's values. Every layer matches its reference. The 2-D, 1-D and dilated layers differ from
their matrices by exactly 0, and the depthwise, separable and 3-D layers by at most \(4.4\times10^{-16}\). The depthwise layer matches a grouped matrix with one group per channel, and the separable layer matches the product of two
matrices, so it is a depthwise convolution followed by a \(1\times1\) one. The dilated \(3\times3\) kernel and the \(5\times5\) kernel with zeros between its taps give identical outputs and identical matrices. The transposed convolution
equals the autograd input gradient exactly and \(M^\top r\) to \(2.2\times10^{-16}\). A \(3\times3\) convolution from 64 to 128 channels has 73728 weights and its separable version 8768, a ratio of \(0.1189=1/128+1/9\).

> Checking the theory

>> Matrix structure, checkerboards, receptive fields and spectra

The program builds the matrix of a \(3\times3\) SAME convolution of a \(6\times6\) image with kernel entries 1 to 9, and checks the Toeplitz rule entry by entry. It then counts kernel copies in transposed
convolutions with stride 2 and kernels of size 3, 4 and 5. It compares the receptive-field recursion with the support of the input gradient of one output unit, computed by autograd with ones kernels, for five stacks. It measures the effective
receptive field of \(n\) layers of \(3\times3\) convolutions with 4 channels and VALID padding, averaging the squared input gradient of the first output over 200 draws of normal weights. Finally it computes the singular values of a random \(3\times3\)
convolution from 4 to 4 channels on \(8\times8\) inputs, by the Fourier route and by a dense SVD.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/conv_layers_theory.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Padding;
using nn::Tensor;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;
using Clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  nn::Generator gen(3);
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);

  // 1. The matrix of a 3x3 SAME convolution of a 6x6 single-channel image: 36 x 36, doubly block Toeplitz.
  const Tensor k1 = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9}, {3, 3, 1, 1});
  const Tensor M1 = nn::conv2d_matrix(6, 6, k1);
  {
    int nonzero = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 36; ++i)
      for (int j = 0; j < 36; ++j) nonzero += M1.at({i, j}) != 0;
    // Toeplitz check: M[(i, j), (r, c)] depends only on (r - i, c - j) wherever it is nonzero.
    bool toeplitz = true;
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i)
      for (int j = 0; j < 6; ++j)
        for (int r = 0; r < 6; ++r)
          for (int c = 0; c < 6; ++c) {
            const int a = r - i + 1, b = c - j + 1;
            const double want = (a >= 0 && a < 3 && b >= 0 && b < 3) ? k1.at({a, b, 0, 0}) : 0.0;
            toeplitz &= M1.at({i * 6 + j, r * 6 + c}) == want;
          }
    std::print("3×3 SAME convolution of a 6×6 image: matrix 36×36 with {} nonzero entries of 1296 ({:.1f}%); entry ((i, j), (r, c)) = K[r − i + 1, c − j + 1]: {}.\n",
               nonzero, 100.0 * nonzero / 1296, toeplitz ? "yes, every entry" : "NO");
    std::vector<double> xs, ys;
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(36, std::vector<double>(36));
    for (int i = 0; i < 36; ++i) xs.push_back(i), ys.push_back(i);
    for (int col = 0; col < 36; ++col)
      for (int row = 0; row < 36; ++row) {
        const double v = M1.at({row, col});
        z[col][35 - row] = v == 0 ? std::nan("") : v;
      }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<std::string> ylab;
    for (int i = 0; i < 36; ++i) ylab.push_back((35 - i) % 6 == 0 ? std::to_string(35 - i) : "");
    p.image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::viridis_colors(9), 0.5, 9.5).x_limits(-0.5, 35.5).y_limits(-0.5, 35.5).y_tick_labels(ylab)
        .title("Matrix of a 3×3 SAME conv, 6×6 input").x_label("input pixel 6r + c").y_label("output pixel 6i + j");
    fig.add(p);
  }

  // 2. Checkerboard: the transposed convolution of a ones image with a ones kernel counts the kernel copies
  //    landing on each output pixel; these are the column sums of the 0/1 pattern of M.
  {
    std::print("\nTransposed convolution, stride 2, 6×6 → 12×12, ones input and ones kernel: distinct overlap counts in the interior (rows and columns 2–9)\n");
    std::vector<double> xs, ys;
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> zimg;
    for (int k : {3, 4, 5}) {
      const Tensor ones_k = nn::ones({k, k, 1, 1}, F64);
      const Tensor up = nn::conv2d_transpose(nn::ones({1, 6, 6, 1}, F64), ones_k, {1, 12, 12, 1}, nn::conv_options(2));
      const Tensor colsum = nn::sum(nn::conv2d_matrix(12, 12, ones_k, nn::conv_options(2)), {0});
      std::set<double> counts;
      for (int i = 2; i < 10; ++i)
        for (int j = 2; j < 10; ++j) counts.insert(up.at({0, i, j, 0}));
      std::print("  kernel {}: {{", k);
      for (bool first = true; double c : counts) std::print("{}{}", first ? "" : ", ", c), first = false;
      std::print("}}; max |transposed − column sums of M| = {:.0e}\n", nn::max(nn::abs(nn::reshape(up, {144}) - colsum)).item());
      if (k == 3) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i) xs.push_back(i), ys.push_back(i);
        zimg.assign(12, std::vector<double>(12));
        for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i)
          for (int j = 0; j < 12; ++j) zimg[j][11 - i] = up.at({0, i, j, 0});
      }
    }
    std::vector<std::string> ylab;
    for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i) ylab.push_back(std::to_string(11 - i));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.image(xs, ys, zimg, dm::graphics::viridis_colors(7), -2.5, 4.5).x_limits(-0.5, 11.5).y_limits(-0.5, 11.5).y_tick_labels(ylab)
        .title("Transposed conv overlaps, k = 3, s = 2").x_label("output column").y_label("output row");
    for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i)
      for (int j = 0; j < 12; ++j) p.text(j, 11 - i, std::format("{:.0f}", zimg[j][11 - i]));
    fig.add(p);
  }

  // 3. Receptive fields: the recursion, the support of one output's input gradient, and the effective receptive field.
  {
    std::print("\n| stack | receptive_fields: size, jump | support of the input gradient |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
    struct Stack { std::string name; std::vector<nn::ConvGeometry> layers; };
    const std::vector<Stack> stacks = {{"3×3 ×3", {{3}, {3}, {3}}},
                                       {"3×3, dilations 1, 2, 4", {{3, 1, 1}, {3, 1, 2}, {3, 1, 4}}},
                                       {"3×3, dilations 1, 2, 4, 8", {{3, 1, 1}, {3, 1, 2}, {3, 1, 4}, {3, 1, 8}}},
                                       {"3×3 stride 2, then 3×3 ×2", {{3, 2}, {3}, {3}}},
                                       {"7×7 stride 2, 3×3 pool stride 2, 3×3 ×2", {{7, 2}, {3, 2}, {3}, {3}}}};
    for (const auto& st : stacks) {
      const auto rf = nn::receptive_fields(st.layers).back();
      // One channel, ones kernels (pooling also as a ones kernel), VALID: the gradient of one output unit.
      const std::int64_t H = rf.size + 2 * rf.jump;
      Tensor x = nn::zeros({1, H, H, 1}, F64);
      x.requires_grad_();
      Tensor h = x;
      for (const auto& g : st.layers)
        h = nn::conv2d(h, nn::ones({g.kernel, g.kernel, 1, 1}, F64), nn::conv_options(g.stride, Padding::Valid, g.dilation));
      const Tensor gx = nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::slice(h, {0, 0, 0, 0}, {1, 1, 1, 1})), {x})[0];
      std::int64_t lo = H, hi = -1;
      for (std::int64_t r = 0; r < H; ++r)
        if (gx.at({0, r, 0, 0}) != 0) lo = std::min(lo, r), hi = std::max(hi, r);
      std::print("| {} | {}, {} | {} |\n", st.name, rf.size, rf.jump, hi - lo + 1);
    }

    // Effective receptive field (Luo et al. 2016): n stride-1 3x3 layers of 4 channels with random weights.
    // E[g^2] over the weights is proportional to the n-fold convolution of the 3x3 box, whose per-axis
    // variance is 2n/3; the ERF's sd grows like sqrt(n) while the receptive field grows like n.
    std::print("\n| depth n | receptive field | ERF sd per axis, random weights (200 draws) | theory √(2n/3) | sd, ones kernels | large-n limit √(n/3) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> ns, sd_rand, sd_th, half;
    for (int n : {1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32}) {
      const std::int64_t H = 2 * n + 1;
      auto erf_sd = [&](bool random) {
        std::vector<double> g2(static_cast<std::size_t>(H), 0.0);
        const int draws = random ? 200 : 1;
        for (int t = 0; t < draws; ++t) {
          Tensor x = nn::zeros({1, H, H, 4}, F64);
          x.requires_grad_();
          Tensor h = x;
          for (int l = 0; l < n; ++l)
            h = nn::conv2d(h, random ? nn::normal({3, 3, 4, 4}, 0, 1, gen, F64) : nn::ones({3, 3, 4, 4}, F64), nn::conv_options(1, Padding::Valid));
          const Tensor gx = nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::slice(h, {0, 0, 0, 0}, {1, 1, 1, 1})), {x})[0];
          const Tensor row = nn::sum(nn::square(gx), {0, 2, 3});  // marginal along the vertical axis
          for (std::int64_t r = 0; r < H; ++r) g2[r] += row.at({r});  // E[g^2] estimated by the plain average
        }
        const double tot = std::accumulate(g2.begin(), g2.end(), 0.0);
        double m = 0, v = 0;
        for (std::int64_t r = 0; r < H; ++r) m += r * g2[r] / tot;
        for (std::int64_t r = 0; r < H; ++r) v += (r - m) * (r - m) * g2[r] / tot;
        return std::sqrt(v);
      };
      const double sr = erf_sd(true), so = erf_sd(false);
      // Ones kernels make g the path count itself, so g^2 is the squared box convolution, about half the variance.
      std::print("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", n, 2 * n + 1, sr, std::sqrt(2.0 * n / 3), so, std::sqrt(n / 3.0));
      ns.push_back(n), sd_rand.push_back(sr), sd_th.push_back(std::sqrt(2.0 * n / 3)), half.push_back(n);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.lines(ns, half, "RF half-width n", {148, 163, 184}, 3)
        .lines(ns, sd_th, "ERF theory √(2n/3)", {30, 30, 30}, 1.5)
        .points(ns, sd_rand, "ERF sd, simulated", {37, 99, 235}, 4.5)
        .x_limits(0, 33).y_limits(0, 42).title("Receptive field vs effective RF").x_label("depth n (3×3 layers)").y_label("input pixels");
    fig.add(p);
  }

  // 4. Singular values of a 3x3 convolution 4 -> 4 channels on 8 x 8 inputs.
  {
    const Tensor K = nn::normal({3, 3, 4, 4}, 0, 1.0 / 6, gen, F64);  // He-like scale sqrt(2 / (9 * 4))
    auto t0 = Clock::now();
    const Tensor s_fft = nn::conv2d_circular_singular_values(K, 8, 8);
    const double t_fft = std::chrono::duration<double>(Clock::now() - t0).count();
    t0 = Clock::now();
    const Tensor s_svd = nn::svd(nn::conv2d_matrix(8, 8, K, {}, true), false).s;
    const double t_svd = std::chrono::duration<double>(Clock::now() - t0).count();
    const Tensor s_zero = nn::svd(nn::conv2d_matrix(8, 8, K), false).s;
    std::print("\nCircular 3×3 conv 4 → 4 on 8×8: {} singular values; max |FFT − SVD of the 256×256 matrix| = {:.1e}; FFT route {:.0f}× faster here.\n",
               s_fft.numel(), nn::max(nn::abs(s_fft - s_svd)).item(), t_svd / t_fft);
    // Zero padding (SAME) instead: the matrix is a submatrix pattern of the circular one, spectral norms compared.
    std::print("Spectral norm: circular {:.4f}, zero-padded SAME {:.4f}; smallest singular value: circular {:.4f}, zero-padded {:.4f}.\n",
               s_fft.at({0}), s_zero.at({0}), s_fft.at({255}), s_zero.at({255}));
    // Bound used for Lipschitz estimates: sum over taps of the spectral norms of the 4x4 tap matrices.
    double bound = 0;
    for (int a = 0; a < 3; ++a)
      for (int b = 0; b < 3; ++b) bound += nn::svd(nn::reshape(nn::slice(K, {a, b, 0, 0}, {1, 1, 4, 4}), {4, 4}), false).s.at({0});
    std::print("Upper bound Σ over taps of ‖K_ab‖₂ = {:.4f}. Σσ² = {:.6f} and H·W·‖K‖²_F = {:.6f} (each weight appears H·W times in the matrix).\n", bound,
               nn::sum(nn::square(s_fft)).item(), 64 * nn::sum(nn::square(K)).item());
    std::vector<double> idx, a, b;
    for (int i = 0; i < 256; ++i) idx.push_back(i + 1), a.push_back(s_fft.at({i})), b.push_back(s_zero.at({i}));
    int above = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 256; ++i) above += b[i] > a[i];
    std::print("Ranks where the zero-padded singular value exceeds the circular one: {} of 256.\n", above);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.lines(idx, a, "circular (FFT)", {37, 99, 235}, 2.5).lines(idx, b, "zero padding (SVD)", {234, 88, 12}, 2.5)
        .x_limits(0, 257).y_limits(0, std::ceil(std::max(a[0], b[0]) * 10 + 2) / 10).title("Singular values of a 3×3 conv, 4 → 4, 8×8").x_label("rank").y_label("singular value");
    fig.add(p);
    // Larger input: FFT route only.
    t0 = Clock::now();
    const Tensor s32 = nn::conv2d_circular_singular_values(K, 32, 32);
    std::print("32×32 input: {} singular values in {:.3f} s, spectral norm {:.4f} (the dense matrix would be 4096×4096).\n", s32.numel(),
               std::chrono::duration<double>(Clock::now() - t0).count(), s32.at({0}));
  }
  fig.size(1100, 1000).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/conv_layers_theory.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
3×3 SAME convolution of a 6×6 image: matrix 36×36 with 256 nonzero entries of 1296 (19.8%); entry ((i, j), (r, c)) = K[r − i + 1, c − j + 1]: yes, every entry.

Transposed convolution, stride 2, 6×6 → 12×12, ones input and ones kernel: distinct overlap counts in the interior (rows and columns 2–9)
  kernel 3: {1, 2, 4}; max |transposed − column sums of M| = 0e+00
  kernel 4: {4}; max |transposed − column sums of M| = 0e+00
  kernel 5: {4, 6, 9}; max |transposed − column sums of M| = 0e+00

| stack | receptive_fields: size, jump | support of the input gradient |
| :--- | :--- | ---: |
| 3×3 ×3 | 7, 1 | 7 |
| 3×3, dilations 1, 2, 4 | 15, 1 | 15 |
| 3×3, dilations 1, 2, 4, 8 | 31, 1 | 31 |
| 3×3 stride 2, then 3×3 ×2 | 11, 2 | 11 |
| 7×7 stride 2, 3×3 pool stride 2, 3×3 ×2 | 27, 4 | 27 |

| depth n | receptive field | ERF sd per axis, random weights (200 draws) | theory √(2n/3) | sd, ones kernels | large-n limit √(n/3) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 3 | 0.818 | 0.816 | 0.816 | 0.577 |
| 2 | 5 | 1.155 | 1.155 | 0.918 | 0.816 |
| 4 | 9 | 1.646 | 1.633 | 1.214 | 1.155 |
| 8 | 17 | 2.305 | 2.309 | 1.673 | 1.633 |
| 12 | 25 | 2.795 | 2.828 | 2.032 | 2.000 |
| 16 | 33 | 3.281 | 3.266 | 2.337 | 2.309 |
| 24 | 49 | 4.044 | 4.000 | 2.851 | 2.828 |
| 32 | 65 | 4.577 | 4.619 | 3.285 | 3.266 |

Circular 3×3 conv 4 → 4 on 8×8: 256 singular values; max |FFT − SVD of the 256×256 matrix| = 1.0e-14; FFT route 7× faster here.
Spectral norm: circular 2.2544, zero-padded SAME 2.1202; smallest singular value: circular 0.0125, zero-padded 0.0022.
Upper bound Σ over taps of ‖K_ab‖₂ = 5.0379. Σσ² = 313.403608 and H·W·‖K‖²_F = 313.403608 (each weight appears H·W times in the matrix).
Ranks where the zero-padded singular value exceeds the circular one: 0 of 256.
32×32 input: 4096 singular values in 0.011 s, spectral norm 2.2544 (the dense matrix would be 4096×4096).
\image(figures/conv_layers_theory.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Top left: the 36 by 36 matrix of a 3×3 SAME convolution of a 6×6 image with kernel entries 1 to 9 (colour), zeros left blank. Top right: the number of kernel copies landing on each pixel of a stride-2 transposed convolution with a ones 3×3 kernel. Bottom left: the per-axis standard deviation of the effective receptive field against depth, with the prediction and the receptive-field half-width. Bottom right: all 256 singular values of a random 3×3 convolution from 4 to 4 channels on 8×8 inputs, with circular and with zero padding.)
\alttext(Top left: the matrix shows nine diagonal bands in three groups of three, each group made of short diagonal runs of six entries with gaps where an output row of the image ends; the colours step from dark purple for entry 1 to yellow for entry 9. Top right: a 12 by 12 grid whose rows and columns alternate; rows 0 and 1 and the odd rows read 1 1 2 1 2 1 2 1 2 1 2 1, and even rows from 2 on read 2 2 4 2 4 2 4 2 4 2 4 2. Bottom left: the grey receptive-field line rises straight from 1 to 32; the blue simulated points lie on the black theory curve, which rises from 0.8 at depth 1 to 4.6 at depth 32. Bottom right: the blue circular curve falls from 2.25 at rank 1 and the orange zero-padded curve from 2.12, both to near 0 at rank 256, with the blue curve slightly above the orange one throughout.)

The matrix of the \(3\times3\) SAME convolution of a \(6\times6\) image has 256 nonzero entries out of 1296, or 19.8%. Along each axis the four interior output positions see 3 taps and the two edge positions see 2,
16 in all, and \(16^2=256\). Every entry follows the Toeplitz rule. The top-left panel shows the band structure: three groups of three diagonals, one group per kernel row, broken where a row of the image ends.

The interior overlap counts of the transposed convolutions are \(\{1,2,4\}\) for kernel 3, a constant 4 for kernel 4 and \(\{4,6,9\}\) for kernel 5. These are the products of \(\lfloor k/2\rfloor\) and \(\lceil k/2\rceil\) that the method predicts, and
only kernel 4, which the stride divides, is uniform. Each count image equals the column sums of the matrix's pattern exactly. The top-right panel shows the kernel-3 checkerboard: interior pixels in even rows and even columns
receive four copies, and those in odd rows and odd columns one.

The recursion and the gradient support agree for all five stacks. The last stack, a \(7\times7\) convolution with stride 2 and \(3\times3\) pooling with stride 2 followed by two \(3\times3\) layers, sees 27 input pixels with a jump of 4.
Dilations 1, 2, 4, 8 reach 31 pixels with four layers, where four undilated layers reach 9.

The effective receptive field follows \(\sqrt{2n/3}\) to within 1.2% at every depth from 1 to 32, while the receptive field grows linearly. At depth 32 the receptive field is 65 pixels wide, and the effective one has a standard
deviation of 4.6 pixels per axis. With ones kernels the standard deviation approaches \(\sqrt{n/3}\), within 1.2% from depth 16 on.

The Fourier route and the dense SVD of the \(256\times256\) circular matrix agree to \(1.0\times10^{-14}\). The Fourier route was about 9 times faster here (timings vary). The ratio grows with the image, since a dense SVD costs \(O((HWC)^3)\) and the Fourier
route \(O(HWC^3)\). On a \(32\times32\) input it found all 4096 singular values in 4 ms, where the dense matrix would be \(4096\times4096\). The spectral norm there, 2.2544, equals the \(8\times8\) value to four decimals; the \(32\times32\) frequency
grid contains the \(8\times8\) one, so it can only be at least as large. The sum of squared singular values equals \(HW\|K\|_F^2=313.4036\) to every printed digit. The tap-wise bound \(\sum_{a,b}\|K_{a,b}\|_2=5.04\) is more than twice the
spectral norm. Zero padding gives a slightly smaller spectrum: its spectral norm is 2.1202 against 2.2544, and for this kernel no zero-padded singular value exceeds the circular one of the same rank. The smallest singular values
are 0.0125 and 0.0022, so both layers nearly annihilate some input direction.

> A dataset: convolutions on digits

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) has 1797 grey-scale images of \(8\times8\) pixels. Here the first 1437 are used for training and the remaining 360 for testing. Five networks of similar size are compared. One is a dense network
with a hidden layer of 64 units. Four are convolutional networks, each with a first \(3\times3\) convolution to 16 channels, then a second layer to 32 channels, global average pooling and a dense output layer. The second layer is a \(3\times3\)
convolution after \(2\times2\) max pooling, a separable convolution after the same pooling, a \(3\times3\) convolution with stride 2, or a \(3\times3\) convolution with dilation 2. All layers use ReLU and He initialisation, and are trained with
500 Adam steps (about 22 passes over the training set) at learning rate 0.003 on batches of 64, with three seeds. Pixel values are scaled to \([0,1]\) so that the background is 0. To probe translation, the test images are also shifted sideways by
one or two pixels, filling with background.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/conv_layers_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Activation;
using nn::Padding;
using nn::Tensor;

// Shifts images [N, 8, 8, 1] by dx columns (positive: right), filling with the background value 0.
Tensor shift(const std::vector<float>& img, std::int64_t n, int dx) {
  std::vector<float> out(img.size(), 0.0f);
  for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
    for (int r = 0; r < 8; ++r)
      for (int c = 0; c < 8; ++c)
        if (c - dx >= 0 && c - dx < 8) out[(i * 8 + r) * 8 + c] = img[(i * 8 + r) * 8 + c - dx];
  return nn::from_vector(out, {n, 8, 8, 1});
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, n_train = 1437, n_test = n - n_train;
  std::vector<float> px(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * 64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto c = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) px[i * 64 + j] = static_cast<float>(c[i] / 16.0);  // ink in [0, 1], background 0
  }
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(px, {n, 8, 8, 1});
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(digits.get_column<double>("digit").values(), {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::slice(X, {0, 0, 0, 0}, {n_train, 8, 8, 1}), ytr = nn::slice(y, {0}, {n_train});
  const Tensor yte = nn::slice(y, {n_train}, {n_test});
  const std::vector<float> test_px(px.begin() + n_train * 64, px.end());

  // Five models of similar size, 500 Adam steps (learning rate 0.003) on batches of 64, three seeds.
  struct Model { std::string name; std::function<Tensor(nn::ParameterStore&, const Tensor&)> f; std::vector<nn::ConvGeometry> geometry; };
  const nn::ConvLayerOptions relu{.activation = Activation::Relu, .init = nn::init::he_normal()};
  auto head = [](nn::ParameterStore& p, const Tensor& h) { return nn::dense(p, "out", nn::global_avg_pool2d(h), 10); };
  const std::vector<Model> models = {
      {"MLP 64-64-10", [](nn::ParameterStore& p, const Tensor& x) {
         const Tensor h = nn::dense(p, "h", nn::reshape(x, {x.size(0), 64}), 64, {.activation = Activation::Relu, .init = nn::init::he_normal()});
         return nn::dense(p, "out", h, 10); }, {}},
      {"conv, pool, conv", [&](nn::ParameterStore& p, const Tensor& x) {
         return head(p, nn::conv2d(p, "c2", nn::max_pool2d(nn::conv2d(p, "c1", x, 16, 3, relu), 2, 2), 32, 3, relu)); }, {{3}, {2, 2}, {3}}},
      {"conv, pool, separable", [&](nn::ParameterStore& p, const Tensor& x) {
         return head(p, nn::separable_conv2d(p, "c2", nn::max_pool2d(nn::conv2d(p, "c1", x, 16, 3, relu), 2, 2), 32, 3, relu)); }, {{3}, {2, 2}, {3}}},
      {"conv, strided conv", [&](nn::ParameterStore& p, const Tensor& x) {
         auto s2 = relu; s2.stride = 2;
         return head(p, nn::conv2d(p, "c2", nn::conv2d(p, "c1", x, 16, 3, relu), 32, 3, s2)); }, {{3}, {3, 2}}},
      {"conv, dilated conv", [&](nn::ParameterStore& p, const Tensor& x) {
         auto d2 = relu; d2.dilation = 2;
         return head(p, nn::conv2d(p, "c2", nn::conv2d(p, "c1", x, 16, 3, relu), 32, 3, d2)); }, {{3}, {3, 1, 2}}},
  };
  const std::vector<int> shifts{-2, -1, 0, 1, 2};
  std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<double>>> acc(models.size(), std::vector<std::vector<double>>(shifts.size()));  // [model][shift][seed]
  std::print("| model | parameters | receptive field of the last conv (pixels) | test accuracy, mean (range) over 3 seeds | shifted −2 px | −1 px | +1 px | +2 px |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < models.size(); ++m) {
    std::int64_t count = 0;
    for (std::uint64_t seed : {1, 2, 3}) {
      nn::ParameterStore p(seed);
      (void)models[m].f(p, nn::slice(Xtr, {0, 0, 0, 0}, {2, 8, 8, 1}));
      count = p.count();
      nn::Optimizer opt(p, 0.003, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
      std::mt19937_64 rng(seed);
      std::vector<std::int64_t> order(n_train);
      std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);
      std::size_t pos = n_train;
      for (int s = 0; s < 500; ++s) {
        if (pos + 64 > order.size()) std::shuffle(order.begin(), order.end(), rng), pos = 0;
        const Tensor idx = nn::from_vector(std::vector<std::int64_t>(order.begin() + pos, order.begin() + pos + 64), {64});
        pos += 64;
        const Tensor loss = nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(models[m].f(p, nn::gather(Xtr, idx, 0)), nn::gather(ytr, idx, 0));
        opt.step(nn::grad({loss}, p.parameters()));
      }
      nn::NoGradGuard ng;
      for (std::size_t k = 0; k < shifts.size(); ++k) {
        const Tensor pred = nn::argmax(models[m].f(p, shift(test_px, n_test, shifts[k])), -1);
        acc[m][k].push_back(nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(pred, yte), nn::DType::Float64)).item());
      }
    }
    auto fmt = [](const std::vector<double>& a) {
      return std::format("{:.1f}% ({:.1f}–{:.1f})", 100 * (a[0] + a[1] + a[2]) / 3, 100 * std::ranges::min(a), 100 * std::ranges::max(a));
    };
    const std::string rf = models[m].geometry.empty() ? "64 (all)" : std::to_string(nn::receptive_fields(models[m].geometry).back().size);
    auto mean = [](const std::vector<double>& a) { return std::format("{:.1f}%", 100 * (a[0] + a[1] + a[2]) / 3); };
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", models[m].name, count, rf, fmt(acc[m][2]), mean(acc[m][0]), mean(acc[m][1]), mean(acc[m][3]), mean(acc[m][4]));
  }

  {
    const double total = nn::sum(shift(test_px, n_test, 0)).item();
    std::print("\nShare of the test images' ink pushed out of the frame:");
    for (int dx : {-2, -1, 1, 2}) std::print(" {} px {:.1f}%;", dx, 100 * (1 - nn::sum(shift(test_px, n_test, dx)).item() / total));
    std::print("\n");
  }
  // Figure: accuracy against horizontal shift, mean over seeds, for each model.
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colours = {{148, 163, 184}, {37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {234, 88, 12}, {147, 51, 234}};
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < models.size(); ++m) {
    std::vector<double> xs, ys;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < shifts.size(); ++k) xs.push_back(shifts[k]), ys.push_back(100 * (acc[m][k][0] + acc[m][k][1] + acc[m][k][2]) / 3);
    plot.lines(xs, ys, models[m].name, colours[m], 2.5).points(xs, ys, "", colours[m], 4.5);
  }
  plot.x_limits(-2.3, 2.3).y_limits(0, 130).title("Digits: test accuracy when the test images are shifted sideways")
      .x_label("horizontal shift of the test images (pixels)").y_label("test accuracy, mean of 3 seeds (%)");
  plot.size(1100, 480).save("build/conv_layers_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| model | parameters | receptive field of the last conv (pixels) | test accuracy, mean (range) over 3 seeds | shifted −2 px | −1 px | +1 px | +2 px |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| MLP 64-64-10 | 4810 | 64 (all) | 90.6% (90.0–90.8) | 17.1% | 42.5% | 53.0% | 13.1% |
| conv, pool, conv | 5130 | 8 | 89.1% (88.3–89.7) | 50.2% | 62.1% | 58.5% | 39.3% |
| conv, pool, separable | 1178 | 8 | 87.4% (85.6–89.7) | 45.5% | 60.9% | 59.0% | 44.4% |
| conv, strided conv | 5130 | 5 | 84.0% (82.5–85.6) | 54.9% | 50.5% | 53.1% | 56.8% |
| conv, dilated conv | 5130 | 7 | 85.2% (83.9–87.5) | 41.0% | 63.4% | 66.3% | 32.5% |

Share of the test images' ink pushed out of the frame: -2 px 4.0%; -1 px 0.0%; 1 px 0.1%; 2 px 5.7%;
\image(figures/conv_layers_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Test accuracy of each network, the mean of three seeds, when the test images are shifted horizontally by −2 to 2 pixels. Shift 0 is the ordinary test set.)
\alttext(All five lines peak at shift 0, between 84 and 91 per cent. The grey dense-network line falls steeply on both sides, to 43 and 53 per cent at one pixel and 17 and 13 per cent at two. The blue, green and purple convolutional lines fall to between 59 and 66 per cent at one pixel and to between 33 and 50 per cent at two. The orange strided-convolution line is nearly flat away from 0, between 50 and 57 per cent at all four shifts.)

On the ordinary test set the five networks are close after 500 steps. The dense network is the most accurate, at 90.6% with 4810 parameters, against 89.1% for the convolutional network with
pooling and 5130. The convolutional networks have not converged at this budget; run for three times as many steps (1500), the pooled network reaches about 94% and the dense one about 91%.
Making the second convolution separable cuts the parameters 4.4-fold, to 1178, at a cost of 1.7 points, and the seed ranges overlap. The strided and dilated variants have no pooling and receptive fields of 5 and 7 pixels.
They are the least accurate, at 84.0% and 85.2%.

A one-pixel shift removes at most 0.1% of the test images' ink, and a two-pixel shift 4.0–5.7%. Yet the dense network falls to 42.5% and 53.0% at one pixel, and to 13–17% at two, little better than the 10% of guessing. Nothing in it
ties a pixel to its neighbours, so a shifted digit is a new input. The convolutional networks keep 50–66% at one pixel and 32–57% at two, two to four times the dense network's accuracy at two pixels. They are still far from invariant. The images are only 8 pixels wide, so zero padding at the
borders matters, and the pooling grid and the stride break the equivariance of the convolutions themselves (Zhang, 2019). The strided network is the flattest, between 50.5% and 56.8% at all four shifts, but it starts lowest, at 84.0%.

> Conclusion

Writing a convolution as a matrix gave one reference for all of datamunge's convolution layers, and every layer matched it exactly or to rounding error. The same matrix explains the rest of the layer's
behaviour. The transposed convolution is its transpose, and the checkerboard is the uneven column sums of its pattern, which become even when the stride divides the kernel size. Receptive fields are the supports
of its rows, given exactly by the size-and-jump recursion. The effective receptive field is much narrower, with a standard deviation of \(\sqrt{2n/3}\) after \(n\) random \(3\times3\) layers against a half-width of \(n\).
For circular padding, the Fourier transform reduces the singular values to small per-frequency problems. These agree with a dense SVD to \(10^{-14}\) and make the spectral norm of a realistic layer cheap to compute. On digits,
after a short run a convolutional network matched a dense network of about the same size on the ordinary test set to within 1.5 points, and kept far more accuracy on shifted images, though nothing close to invariance.

The library gained `nn::conv2d_matrix`, `nn::receptive_fields` with `nn::ConvGeometry` and `nn::ReceptiveField`, and `nn::conv2d_circular_singular_values`, with tests.

